it's great to be joined today by Igor  Volsky who's the co-founder and  executive director of guns down America  and author of the book guns down how to  defeat the NRA and build a safer future  with fewer guns  Igor thanks for joining me to talk about  this thank you so much for having me so  to start with one of the things I've  been talking about when it comes to gun  safety regulations is that on the one  hand you have extremely both sort of  fetishized and emotional reactions to  the issue of gun safety from a  significant portion of the population  but at the same time in a lot of places  people are not voting on the basis of  gun safety proposals from candidates so  there's this kind of strange dichotomy  where on the one hand people have  extremely strong views on this issue on  the other hand it's not clear that it's  a voting issue and it leads to I believe  those who are advocating for increased  gun safety regulations at a fundamental  disadvantage do you agree with my sort  of structural framing or disagree  well David one of the most interesting  things I learned when writing guns down  was this new research that analyzed how  the NRA communicated to its members and  what it discovered is that the NRA uses  very emotional terms terms that suggest  a certain identity and so gun ownership  for a lot of the NRA followers or some  portion of the NRA followers isn't just  about the intricacies of policies it's  literally tied in and intertwined with  their identities of who they are  and and the NRA typically describes gun  owners as these good law-abiding  entirely patriotic citizens and of  course describes the other side of this  issue as the folks who want to take guns  away and get government into your life  and it's that kind of frame that they've  been using really since 1977 in a big  way that creates that kind of emotional  connection  and that creates as you point out a  clear connection between the hardcore  NRA members and then how they vote on  this issue so they vote on guns in a way  that the other side doesn't now that's  changing on the gun control side but  it's gonna take some time and of course  the gun control side our side  I'd say talks about this issue in facts  and figures and in all of these wonky  ways that really doesn't resonate as  well as the emotional identity terms  that the NRA uses so I think that's a  big part of why we see that kind of  divide so think back to after the Sandy  Hook shooting there was I mean in a  sense the gun safety advocates more or  less did a lot of the right things there  was a horrible mass shooting there was a  sort of coalescing of opinion that it's  time to at minimum past universal  background checks on any gun transaction  doesn't matter if it's private at a gun  show you just a hundred percent there  was 85 to 90% of agreement on that from  Americans which means that that's that's  some Democrats and some Republicans and  some independents it crosses party lines  and still it wasn't done who and and  when did the failure who failed and why  did that fail to take place even when it  seemed as though the conversation was  going in the right direction  people were relatively United on that  issue what went wrong well look that was  certainly a major disappointment I  remember the day the vote ultimately  failed we were in a restaurant with  survivors who worked so hard to try to  get it across the finish line but in  terms of what actually happened it's an  interesting story because initially  after Newtown there was this real sense  even among Republicans that now with 20  children gunned down six staff members  gunned down in Newtown that something  was actually going to change and you had  Republican members of Congress  particularly in the Senate saying all  right I think I can support this  you had at the very beginning the NRA at  the table helping right what eventually  became the manchin-toomey bill as that  was happening that of course is the bill  that ultimately failed as that was  happening as the manchin-toomey bill was  being put together by senators joe  Manchin and Pat Toomey a Republican and  the Democrats was a bipartisan effort  the gun owners of America which is a  even more conservative gun rights group  than the NRA got wind of the fact that  the NRA was helping put together this  measure and began making a lot of noise  and saying the NRA isn't the real  organization that's protecting your gun  rights it's us gun owners of America and  the NRA never wanting to be outflanked  told back in the entire effort crumbled  and it partly crumbled because of the  pull that the NRA has with Republican  members of Congress to whom it gives  thousands of dollars in contributions  and and who had helps elect and so  that's the dynamic that occurred it  surprised a lot of people that we were  so close to possibly getting something  done there was a true bipartisan effort  and then it crumbled given the internal  politics in the gun rights movement well  it's not even just just the internal  politics because the internal politics  would matter less if in general the gun  lobby had less influence less money and  and less power so what do we do about  that because the geo a versus NRA  dispute is relevant only if as a  lobbying group they have power so I feel  like it's a more systemic problem that  we're dealing with well it is a more  systemic problem but I'll also say that  the gun control movement has really  evolved from where they were in Newtown  to where they are today after Parkland  to a place where you have organizations  like mine guns down America that are  calling for far bolder solutions  to reduce gun deaths that's arguing that  what we really need to do to solve this  problem is to create a future with fewer  guns a future where guns are  significantly harder to get we're  arguing that it's not a problem of the  bad guys having guns but it's the guns  themselves that are the problem and we  need to change the environment for  everybody in which the the environment  in which guns are purchased that needs  to be strengthened the standard for gun  ownership needs to increase that's a big  evolution from where we were in 2013  when the background check bill failed  and it's a real maturity I think in the  gun control movement and it's a change  in dynamic and so I think moving forward  particularly when you saw after parkland  all of those companies I think was about  40 companies that broke ties with the  NRA yeah we just launched a campaign  this morning in the New York Times  that's gonna push the largest public  facing banks to stop doing business with  the gun industry until that industry is  fundamentally reformed that's a new area  of advocacy that seeks to drain the gun  industry seeks to drain the NRA that did  not exist in Newtown so I'm actually  fairly optimistic I mean I take your  point about the dynamics but I think the  movement is so much stronger today in  2019 than it was in 2013 so let's talk  about some let's drill into like one  nitty gritty element of this that I  think the gun safety crowd is not always  completely united on not because of  anything other than it's a complex issue  which is let's talk about types of  firearms okay we know that as a few data  points on the one hand a lot of the more  publicized mass shootings involve what  are called assault style rifles we know  that statistically most gun homicides  are actually handguns in situations  where one or two people are either  injured or killed then there is the  counter to that which is yes but a lot  of those are suicides and it's not  actually people going out and harming  others what do you recommend  then you outlined this new second  amendment compact which we can sort of  talk about in cut this context what is  your suggestion as to what the gun  safety advocate position should be on  types of firearms well first David let  me say you keep on saying gun safety I  would suggest the term gun control  because guns are inherently dangerous  really in anyone's hands which is why  I'm suggesting we increase the standard  for gun ownership  I want to control guns now I find a way  to make those guns safer well I think  it's about when I say gun safety I mean  make make the country safer that's fair  that's fair in terms of what I want to  see and the new second amendment compact  you know they're I think 13 in the in  the final version different policies  that I put forward but it's really  easier to break them up into three  different buckets and all of those  buckets I'll say point towards a single  long-term goal of building a future with  fewer guns and and they look like this  and they go after by the way as you  point out the homicides the suicides  than the mass shootings so bucket number  one is really regulating the gun  industry in a real way right now the  products that they produce are entirely  unregulated  and what happened was in the late 1980s  and early 90s the gun industry made a  decision to begin producing militarized  weapons for the civilian market that's  how you got the assault weapons but just  as importantly that's how you got  militarized handguns that use larger  rounds those rounds are coming at you  faster and what we're seeing in some  cities across America is that folks are  dying from bullet wounds that they were  surviving with past generations of guns  that's a big problem that needs to  change and the reason why they're exempt  by the way is because of an amendment  that the late John Dingell put forward  now his wife who holds his seat wants to  plug that hole she has legislate  to do that that's a good idea so that's  bucket number one really cracking down  on the industry it also of course  includes repealing placa which is that  law that gives broad immunity to into  two to the gun manufacturers and to  dealers bucket two focuses on making  guns significantly harder to get that's  where I talked about raising the bar for  gun ownership and what I mean by that is  if you choose to own a gun you should  have to go to your local police  department get fingerprinted pass a  written test pass a field test pass a  comprehensive background check get  insurance on the firearm you're gonna  purchase have a waiting period between  when you apply for the license and then  when you get the license and then only  then can you get a gun and the old side  then have to register that gun  now there's a system in Massachusetts  that's very similar to what I'm  describing I was going to mention that  here in Boston if I want a firearm I  actually have to go and meet with the  police and demonstrate to their  satisfaction that I have a particular  particular need for its Otis yeah and  that's been a terrific model not just in  terms of lowering homicides but you also  point to suicides right that's two  thirds of our gun deaths we know from  research that people commit suicides  during a short window of crisis and so  getting  extending the length of time between  when you want a gun and when you  purchase a gun get some out of that  crisis window and saves lives yeah of  course people will say that some of  those people who are truly committed to  taking their life at that time if  there's a waiting period they'll find  some other mechanism to take their life  and of course with some people that will  be true but not everybody not everybody  and you are going to save some people  yeah I mean you know there's a little  literature literature on suicide is very  very rich and and statistically speaking  folks who try to commit suicide once and  are unsuccessful are in a period of  crisis when they're thinking of  committing suicide they are far less  likely to try again so yes some people  or determined will of course find  another way but what we also know is  that the suicide by gun is far more  fatal than suicide by any other means  combined so the numbers on that are on  that are very very strong and then the  third piece the third bucket really  deals with some of the gun violence we  have in places like Chicago and  Baltimore in Philadelphia what we have  in those cities are very specific areas  and very specific individuals that you  could literally circle on a map and  figure out where they are street by  street block by block and those folks  are responsible for the overwhelming  majority of the gun deaths that are  happening in those cities and since  about the 1990s what we've had are  community based violence intervention  programs that are operating in all of in  many of these cities that use folks from  the community to help identify who these  individuals are and to de-escalate the  you know the beefs that they might have  away from guns and into and channel that  into more constructive forms of problem  solving and really changing the norms of  the community and that has been  incredibly successful we've seen success  rates of about thirty to seventy percent  of reducing gun deaths in urban areas  those programs are important and they  really need to be supported with federal  dollars the book is guns down how to  defeat the NRA and build a safer future  with fewer guns we've been speaking with  the book's author Igor Volsky who's also  the co-founder and executive director of  guns down America  Igor thanks so much for talking to us  about this today thanks so much David

